What is Rollback in Git :
        Rollback in Git is the process of reverting the project to a previous commit in order to undo unwanted changes or fix issues. 
        It helps restore the code to a stable version.
Other Definition : 
        Rollback in Git is the process of restoring the code to a previous stable commit using commands like git reset, git restore, or git revert. 
        In team environments, git revert is preferred because it maintains history.

Why Do We Need Rollback : 
In real projects: A new feature breaks production
                  A wrong commit is pushed
                  Bug introduced accidentally

Instead of manually fixing everything, we rollback to a stable commit.

Ways to Perform Rollback in Git
There are multiple methods depending on situation 👇

1. git restore (Undo file changes) - Undo changes in working directory:
                                     git restore filename
                                     Used before committing.

2. git reset (Move HEAD back) - Used to undo commits.
          1. Soft Reset (keeps changes staged) - [ git reset --soft HEAD~1 ]
          2. Mixed Reset (default – keeps changes unstaged) - [ git reset HEAD~1 ]
          3.Hard Reset (removes commit + changes completely) - [ git reset --hard HEAD~1 ]
   
4. git revert (Safest way in team projects) - Creates a new commit that undoes previous commit.
                                              Syntax : git revert commit_id
                                              Safe for shared branches
                                              Keeps history intact

Difference between git reset and git revert

git reset	                        git revert

Removes commit history	          Keeps history
Rewrites history	              Creates new commit
Risky in shared branch	          Safe in shared branch
Used locally	                  Used in production/team


